1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,756
This well has been producing oil for eleven years.

2
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:07,984
Two years ago it made a thousand cubic metres a day.

3
00:00:08,075 --> 00:00:10,010
Today it makes six hundred.

4
00:00:10,101 --> 00:00:16,293
The reservoir is healthy, the pressure is fine, and the
fault is somewhere inside the well itself:

5
00:00:16,323 --> 00:00:22,101
a steel pipe fourteen centimetres wide and four kilometres
long, that nobody can look into.

6
00:00:22,693 --> 00:00:24,278
So what do you do?

7
00:00:24,579 --> 00:00:27,008
You cannot send a person down.

8
00:00:27,099 --> 00:00:32,132
You cannot open it, because it is full of oil at two
hundred bar.

9
00:00:32,223 --> 00:00:38,956
All you can do is lower something into it, on a wire or a
pipe, and work blind.

10
00:00:39,917 --> 00:00:44,455
This is well intervention: going back into a well that is
already built,

11
00:00:44,485 --> 00:00:50,323
and usually still producing, to look inside it, clean it,
repair it, change it, or close it.

12
00:00:50,413 --> 00:00:54,043
It is how the industry keeps wells alive for decades.

13
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:56,827
And it comes down to two questions.

14
00:00:56,917 --> 00:00:59,087
Why do we go back into a well?

15
00:00:59,387 --> 00:01:04,308
And how: on a wire, on a coiled steel tube, or with a rig?

16
00:01:04,608 --> 00:01:07,233
This film answers both, in that order.

17
00:01:14,983 --> 00:01:17,265
First, what are we reaching into?

18
00:01:17,565 --> 00:01:20,872
A finished well is built from nested steel.

19
00:01:20,963 --> 00:01:24,853
Casing lines the hole, and cement seals the gap behind it,

20
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:28,262
so nothing can leak between the rock and the well.

21
00:01:28,352 --> 00:01:32,668
Inside hangs the tubing, a narrower pipe that carries the
oil up.

22
00:01:32,758 --> 00:01:36,507
Near the bottom, a packer, a plug of steel and rubber,

23
00:01:36,537 --> 00:01:41,575
seals the gap between tubing and casing, so the oil has
only one way up.

24
00:01:41,665 --> 00:01:46,102
A few hundred metres below the seabed sits the downhole
safety valve,

25
00:01:46,132 --> 00:01:52,412
held open by hydraulic pressure from the surface and built
to slam shut if that pressure is lost.

26
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:57,796
And on the seabed, the Christmas tree: a block of valves
that controls the flow.

27
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:03,408
Look closer at the tubing and you find it is not plain
pipe.

28
00:02:03,499 --> 00:02:06,006
It is a toolbox, built in.

29
00:02:06,098 --> 00:02:12,279
Landing nipples are short sections with a machined groove
inside: a seat where a plug can be locked.

30
00:02:12,371 --> 00:02:18,052
A sliding sleeve is a window in the tubing wall that can
be opened or closed.

31
00:02:18,143 --> 00:02:23,584
Side-pocket mandrels are bulges in the tubing that hold
small valves off to one side.

32
00:02:23,676 --> 00:02:28,541
And at the bottom are the perforations, the holes shot
through casing and cement,

33
00:02:28,571 --> 00:02:31,471
where oil from the rock finally enters the well.

34
00:02:31,563 --> 00:02:38,124
Almost everything we do through the tubing is done to one
of these, or with one of these.

35
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:40,726
Drilling builds the well.

36
00:02:40,816 --> 00:02:46,994
Intervention is everything we do to it afterwards: going
in to measure something, or to change something.

37
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:49,901
You will also hear the word workover.

38
00:02:49,991 --> 00:02:54,708
It is the older term, and it usually means the heavy end
of the job,

39
00:02:54,738 --> 00:02:58,299
where the tubing itself is pulled out and replaced.

40
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:03,210
The industry sorts interventions by how much machinery
they need.

41
00:03:03,302 --> 00:03:06,121
Light intervention goes in through the tree,

42
00:03:06,151 --> 00:03:11,524
on a wireline or a coiled tubing string, with a small
crew, or from a small vessel.

43
00:03:11,616 --> 00:03:15,093
Heavy intervention uses a rig, which removes the tree,

44
00:03:15,123 --> 00:03:19,539
fits a stack of safety valves, and pulls the tubing out of
the hole.

45
00:03:19,631 --> 00:03:22,898
One question separates the two: is the well live?

46
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,503
A live well is full of pressured hydrocarbons that will
flow if you open it.

47
00:03:28,594 --> 00:03:36,361
A killed well is filled with a heavy kill fluid that holds
the pressure back, so it can be opened safely.

48
00:03:36,452 --> 00:03:40,848
Killing a well buys safety, but it costs days, can damage
the rock,

49
00:03:40,878 --> 00:03:44,181
and may leave the well producing less than before.

50
00:03:44,273 --> 00:03:46,923
So whenever we can, we work live.

51
00:03:47,883 --> 00:03:48,895
Why bother?

52
00:03:49,197 --> 00:03:51,579
Because every producing well declines.

53
00:03:51,671 --> 00:03:57,704
As the reservoir is drained its pressure falls, water
arrives, and the rate slides down a curve.

54
00:03:57,796 --> 00:04:00,418
An intervention bends that curve back up.

55
00:04:00,510 --> 00:04:06,144
Its value is the oil you would not otherwise have
produced, minus what the job cost.

56
00:04:06,236 --> 00:04:10,486
And that is why the choice of method matters so much.

57
00:04:10,578 --> 00:04:15,376
A wire unit can be rigged up in hours by a few people.

58
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:21,419
A rig is a floating industrial site that takes weeks and
employs a crew many times larger.

59
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:27,120
The same repair, done the cheap way, can turn a marginal
job into a profitable one.

60
00:04:30,883 --> 00:04:37,880
Wells fail in a handful of ways, so there are only a
handful of reasons to go back in.

61
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:45,017
We will walk through seven: to see, to secure, to clear,
to stimulate, to lift, to steer, and to finish.

62
00:04:45,108 --> 00:04:47,048
For each, ask three questions.

63
00:04:47,139 --> 00:04:48,500
What is wrong?

64
00:04:48,801 --> 00:04:50,185
How would we know?

65
00:04:50,485 --> 00:04:52,265
And what would we do?

66
00:04:53,217 --> 00:04:54,460
First, to see.

67
00:04:54,551 --> 00:04:56,873
A well is a black box.

68
00:04:56,964 --> 00:05:01,177
All we get at the surface is a pressure and a flow rate.

69
00:05:01,267 --> 00:05:03,725
To look inside, we lower instruments.

70
00:05:03,816 --> 00:05:08,014
A gauge records pressure and temperature while the well is
shut in.

71
00:05:08,104 --> 00:05:10,215
This is a pressure build-up test,

72
00:05:10,245 --> 00:05:16,510
and how the pressure builds back tells a reservoir
engineer how easily the rock gives up its oil,

73
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:19,330
and how much damage there is near the well.

74
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:23,730
A production log carries a small spinner, a turbine turned
by the flow,

75
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:30,118
and measures the flow past each perforation: which ones
produce, which are watering out, which are dead.

76
00:05:30,209 --> 00:05:36,109
A caliper feels the tubing wall with thin arms, and finds
corrosion before it becomes a hole.

77
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:39,393
Nearly every other job starts with one of these.

78
00:05:40,350 --> 00:05:42,268
Second, to secure the well.

79
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:48,370
A producing well must keep two independent barriers
between the reservoir and the outside world,

80
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:49,218
at all times.

81
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:50,581
Time attacks them.

82
00:05:50,672 --> 00:05:51,948
Tubing corrodes.

83
00:05:52,039 --> 00:05:53,571
A packer seal leaks.

84
00:05:53,662 --> 00:05:56,336
The safety valve fails its periodic test.

85
00:05:56,427 --> 00:06:02,394
Pressure shows up in the annulus, the gap between tubing
and casing, where there should be none.

86
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:08,632
That is a barrier problem, and the regulations say it must
be understood and dealt with,

87
00:06:08,662 --> 00:06:10,628
or the well must be shut in.

88
00:06:10,719 --> 00:06:15,238
Interventions find the leak with a log, and then restore
the barrier:

89
00:06:15,268 --> 00:06:19,421
a plug set below it, a patch across it, or a replacement
valve.

90
00:06:20,383 --> 00:06:23,075
Third, to clear what is in the way.

91
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:27,264
Scale is mineral that falls out of the water: barium
sulphate,

92
00:06:27,294 --> 00:06:30,327
where injected seawater meets formation water,

93
00:06:30,357 --> 00:06:35,683
or calcium carbonate, as the pressure drops and carbon
dioxide leaves the water.

94
00:06:35,774 --> 00:06:41,095
Wax comes out of the oil as it cools, and asphaltenes as
it loses pressure.

95
00:06:41,186 --> 00:06:47,673
Hydrates are ice-like solids of water and gas that form
when it is cold and the pressure is high.

96
00:06:47,764 --> 00:06:51,715
Sand and fines settle at the bottom and bury the
perforations.

97
00:06:51,806 --> 00:06:56,704
Each one narrows the flow path, and the rate falls as the
bore closes.

98
00:06:56,795 --> 00:07:02,356
The cure is to scrape it, jet it, mill it, dissolve it, or
wash it out.

99
00:07:02,447 --> 00:07:08,695
And sometimes the obstruction is the debris of an earlier
job, a dropped tool or a broken wire.

100
00:07:08,786 --> 00:07:13,057
That is called a fish, and getting it out is called
fishing.

101
00:07:14,017 --> 00:07:15,526
Fourth, to stimulate.

102
00:07:15,617 --> 00:07:19,976
Drilling mud leaves a skin of solids in the pores around
the well,

103
00:07:20,006 --> 00:07:22,866
and production can leave damage of its own.

104
00:07:22,958 --> 00:07:25,926
It restricts the inflow like a clogged filter.

105
00:07:26,018 --> 00:07:30,842
Acid pumped into the rock dissolves it, and that is called
matrix stimulation.

106
00:07:30,934 --> 00:07:34,988
If the problem is that too little rock is connected to the
well,

107
00:07:35,018 --> 00:07:41,079
we can shoot more perforations, or in some wells crack the
rock open with a hydraulic fracture.

108
00:07:41,171 --> 00:07:45,278
In every case the treatment has to be placed at a precise
depth,

109
00:07:45,308 --> 00:07:49,058
which means pumping through a pipe that can be positioned.

110
00:07:50,017 --> 00:07:51,313
Fifth, to lift.

111
00:07:51,405 --> 00:07:57,668
As the reservoir pressure falls, many oil wells can no
longer push their fluid to the surface unaided.

112
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:01,230
Gas lift fixes that: gas is injected down the annulus,

113
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:07,223
enters the tubing through valves in the side-pocket
mandrels, and lightens the fluid column.

114
00:08:07,315 --> 00:08:09,847
Each valve is a small automatic gate.

115
00:08:09,939 --> 00:08:13,771
A bellows charged with nitrogen holds a ball on its seat,

116
00:08:13,801 --> 00:08:18,476
until the gas pressure in the annulus is high enough to
push it open.

117
00:08:18,567 --> 00:08:21,524
At start-up the valves work from the top down:

118
00:08:21,554 --> 00:08:25,940
each one passes gas until the liquid has been pushed below
the next,

119
00:08:25,970 --> 00:08:30,256
then closes, until gas enters at the deepest, the
operating valve.

120
00:08:30,348 --> 00:08:34,595
A check valve stops well fluid from flowing back into the
annulus.

121
00:08:34,686 --> 00:08:37,495
Valves wear out, and are changed by wire.

122
00:08:37,587 --> 00:08:40,280
In gas wells the opposite problem appears.

123
00:08:40,371 --> 00:08:45,578
As the rate drops, the gas moves too slowly to carry
droplets of water out,

124
00:08:45,608 --> 00:08:49,519
so they fall back and the well drowns in its own liquid.

125
00:08:49,611 --> 00:08:51,248
That is liquid loading.

126
00:08:51,339 --> 00:08:54,961
A narrower string of coiled tubing hung inside the tubing,

127
00:08:54,991 --> 00:08:59,084
a velocity string, speeds the gas up again and restores
the flow.

128
00:09:00,050 --> 00:09:02,711
Sixth, to steer what the well produces.

129
00:09:02,804 --> 00:09:04,869
Reservoirs do not give evenly.

130
00:09:04,961 --> 00:09:09,485
Water arrives early through one zone and drowns the oil
from the others.

131
00:09:09,577 --> 00:09:14,785
The water cut climbs, and the well can die long before the
reservoir is empty.

132
00:09:14,878 --> 00:09:19,673
To fix it we shut the offending zone off, with a plug, a
cement squeeze,

133
00:09:19,703 --> 00:09:24,527
or by closing a sliding sleeve, and perhaps open another
zone higher up.

134
00:09:24,620 --> 00:09:32,188
A few modern wells have valves that do this on command,
but most have to be steered by going in.

135
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:34,510
Seventh, to finish.

136
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:42,422
When a well reaches the end of its life, it must be
permanently plugged, so that it can never leak again.

137
00:09:42,512 --> 00:09:48,798
Before the plugs go in, the well is logged, cleaned out,
and the pipes may be cut.

138
00:09:48,889 --> 00:09:51,973
Some of that work can be done without a rig.

139
00:09:52,064 --> 00:09:57,506
Or the well is not finished, only changed: converted from
producer to water injector,

140
00:09:57,536 --> 00:10:01,270
or a side-track is drilled out of it into fresh reservoir.

141
00:10:01,361 --> 00:10:04,196
Either way, an intervention prepares the ground.

142
00:10:05,150 --> 00:10:08,375
Look at what these seven jobs ask of us.

143
00:10:08,465 --> 00:10:13,237
To see: carry a sensor to a depth, and bring the answer
back.

144
00:10:13,328 --> 00:10:17,616
To secure: set a plug, pull a valve, shift a sleeve.

145
00:10:17,706 --> 00:10:19,726
To clear: scrape, mill, wash.

146
00:10:19,817 --> 00:10:23,058
To stimulate: pump a fluid to an exact spot.

147
00:10:23,149 --> 00:10:26,439
To lift: swap a valve, or hang a pipe.

148
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:29,562
To steer: set a plug, shoot a perforation.

149
00:10:29,653 --> 00:10:35,073
Put it all together and you get a short list of
capabilities: carry and place tools,

150
00:10:35,103 --> 00:10:39,061
pull and hammer, pump, push, rotate, and send power and
data.

151
00:10:39,152 --> 00:10:42,515
Every intervention method is a different bundle of those.

152
00:10:42,606 --> 00:10:44,992
Now we can look at the bundles.

153
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:51,817
The problem with a live well is simple.

154
00:10:51,908 --> 00:10:54,164
Open the top and it flows.

155
00:10:54,256 --> 00:10:57,285
So the tools must go in through an airlock.

156
00:10:57,377 --> 00:11:01,465
A lubricator is a pressure-tight tube bolted on top of the
tree,

157
00:11:01,495 --> 00:11:06,000
long enough to hold the whole toolstring, the tools joined
end to end.

158
00:11:06,092 --> 00:11:11,790
We load the tools into the lubricator while the valve
below it is still shut.

159
00:11:11,881 --> 00:11:18,391
Then we pressure up the lubricator until it matches the
well, so there is no difference across the valve.

160
00:11:18,482 --> 00:11:22,671
Now it can be opened, and the toolstring can drop in.

161
00:11:22,763 --> 00:11:27,376
To come out, the sequence runs backwards: tools up into
the lubricator,

162
00:11:27,406 --> 00:11:30,218
valve closed, lubricator bled down, opened.

163
00:11:30,309 --> 00:11:32,589
The well never meets the atmosphere.

164
00:11:33,550 --> 00:11:41,068
On a platform or a land well, the tree sits at the top of
the well, and it is built from valves.

165
00:11:41,159 --> 00:11:45,989
Two master valves are the main shut-off, one above the
other for redundancy.

166
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:49,975
The wing valve sends the oil off to the pipeline.

167
00:11:50,067 --> 00:11:55,013
And at the very top, straight above the tubing, is the
swab valve.

168
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:56,594
That is our door.

169
00:11:56,686 --> 00:11:59,089
Bolt the lubricator on top of it,

170
00:11:59,119 --> 00:12:05,240
and the swab valve and the master valves are what separate
the tools from the well.

171
00:12:05,332 --> 00:12:12,289
In Norway the regulations require at least two main valves
on a tree, and at least one of them automatic.

172
00:12:13,250 --> 00:12:14,822
Now the hard part.

173
00:12:14,913 --> 00:12:20,523
The lubricator has a top, and the line has to pass through
it, while moving.

174
00:12:20,615 --> 00:12:27,247
Solid wire passes through a stuffing box: rubber packing
squeezed around the wire by hydraulic pressure,

175
00:12:27,277 --> 00:12:30,928
tight enough to seal, loose enough to let the wire slide.

176
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:35,651
Stranded cable has gaps between its strands, so packing
cannot seal on it.

177
00:12:35,742 --> 00:12:38,313
It uses a grease injection head instead.

178
00:12:38,405 --> 00:12:44,151
Grease is pumped at a higher pressure than the well into
narrow tubes around the cable,

179
00:12:44,181 --> 00:12:46,438
and the grease itself is the seal.

180
00:12:46,529 --> 00:12:48,952
Below that sits a blowout preventer,

181
00:12:48,982 --> 00:12:54,841
a stack of valves whose rams can close on the line and
seal around it in an emergency.

182
00:12:54,932 --> 00:13:01,422
And a tool catcher, so that if the line ever breaks, the
toolstring cannot fall back into the well.

183
00:13:02,383 --> 00:13:04,591
There is one more invisible force.

184
00:13:04,683 --> 00:13:07,727
Pressure pushes on anything that enters the well.

185
00:13:07,818 --> 00:13:13,164
A solid wire a little under three millimetres thick, in a
well at two hundred bar,

186
00:13:13,194 --> 00:13:16,734
is pushed out with about a hundred and twenty newtons.

187
00:13:16,825 --> 00:13:19,773
That is the weight of a twelve-kilogram mass.

188
00:13:19,865 --> 00:13:26,406
If the toolstring weighs less than that, the well blows it
back out, and the wire with it.

189
00:13:26,497 --> 00:13:30,161
That is why a wireline toolstring carries a heavy weight.

190
00:13:30,253 --> 00:13:35,200
Now think of a coiled tube, two inches across, in the same
well.

191
00:13:35,291 --> 00:13:41,295
The area is more than three hundred times bigger, and so
is the force: about four tonnes.

192
00:13:41,386 --> 00:13:42,654
Keep that number.

193
00:13:42,745 --> 00:13:45,086
It shapes everything about coiled tubing.

194
00:13:46,050 --> 00:13:48,348
All of this serves one rule.

195
00:13:48,439 --> 00:13:54,636
Whatever we do in a well, there must be two tested
barriers between the reservoir and the outside world:

196
00:13:54,666 --> 00:13:59,515
two independent envelopes of equipment, each able to hold
the pressure by itself.

197
00:13:59,606 --> 00:14:03,930
During a wireline job the safety valve and the tree valves
stand open,

198
00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:07,505
with the line running through them, so they cannot count.

199
00:14:07,596 --> 00:14:10,140
The equipment on top takes their place.

200
00:14:10,231 --> 00:14:15,944
Typically the stuffing box closes the primary envelope at
the top, while the blowout preventer,

201
00:14:15,974 --> 00:14:22,262
which can close on the line, belongs to the secondary, and
elements such as the tree are shared by both.

202
00:14:22,353 --> 00:14:27,248
So everything on the stack is pressure-tested before the
well is opened to it.

203
00:14:27,339 --> 00:14:32,224
Exactly which element belongs to which envelope is set by
the standard,

204
00:14:32,254 --> 00:14:35,784
NORSOK D-010, and by the operator's own procedures.

205
00:14:35,876 --> 00:14:40,193
And if a barrier fails, the job stops until it is
restored.

206
00:14:43,950 --> 00:14:46,419
Wireline is a family of three.

207
00:14:46,509 --> 00:14:51,575
Slickline is a single solid strand of steel, a little
under three millimetres thick.

208
00:14:51,665 --> 00:14:57,540
It is cheap, light and quick to rig up, and it can only do
mechanical work:

209
00:14:57,570 --> 00:15:00,798
pull, push by its own weight, and hammer.

210
00:15:00,889 --> 00:15:04,850
Braided line is stranded, like a rope, which makes it
stronger,

211
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:09,314
and so better for heavy fishing, but it needs the grease
head to seal.

212
00:15:09,405 --> 00:15:15,078
And electric line, or e-line, is braided cable with
insulated conductors at its core.

213
00:15:15,168 --> 00:15:20,564
It carries power down and data up, so it can run logging
tools and perforate.

214
00:15:20,654 --> 00:15:28,430
The thickness of the line decides how hard you can pull,
and so how much trouble you can get out of.

215
00:15:29,383 --> 00:15:31,398
Here is a slickline spread.

216
00:15:31,488 --> 00:15:37,428
The wire is stored on a drum, thousands of metres of it,
driven by a hydraulic motor.

217
00:15:37,518 --> 00:15:43,710
It leaves the drum through a measuring head that counts
how far it has gone and reads the tension,

218
00:15:43,740 --> 00:15:48,820
passes up over a sheave, and drops through the stuffing
box into the lubricator.

219
00:15:48,910 --> 00:15:53,134
And that tension reading is the operator's only window
into the well.

220
00:15:53,224 --> 00:15:55,269
They cannot see the tools.

221
00:15:55,359 --> 00:16:03,065
They feel them: the weight of the string, the snag at a
tight spot, the sudden drop when a pin shears.

222
00:16:04,017 --> 00:16:07,323
On the end of the wire hangs the toolstring.

223
00:16:07,414 --> 00:16:11,238
At the top, a rope socket ties the wire off.

224
00:16:11,329 --> 00:16:13,487
Below it, the stem, or weight bars:

225
00:16:13,517 --> 00:16:19,267
heavy steel to overcome the pressure force and the
friction, and to give mass for hammering.

226
00:16:19,358 --> 00:16:20,630
Then the jars.

227
00:16:20,721 --> 00:16:26,349
If the well deviates, a knuckle joint, a hinge that lets
the string bend round curves.

228
00:16:26,439 --> 00:16:32,397
And at the bottom, the business end: the running tool,
pulling tool or whatever the job needs.

229
00:16:33,350 --> 00:16:34,486
Why jars?

230
00:16:34,786 --> 00:16:41,807
Because the wire can only pull, and often a tool is stuck,
or a pin has to be sheared.

231
00:16:41,897 --> 00:16:48,637
The answer is a jar, a sliding joint with a short stroke,
set between the stem and the tool.

232
00:16:48,727 --> 00:16:52,203
To jar up, the operator pulls the wire in fast.

233
00:16:52,294 --> 00:16:57,056
The stem accelerates through the stroke and slams into the
top of it,

234
00:16:57,086 --> 00:17:01,184
a blow far harder than any steady pull the wire could
hold.

235
00:17:01,274 --> 00:17:05,455
Many jars add a spring catch: it holds until the pull
builds,

236
00:17:05,485 --> 00:17:11,215
the wire stretches like a spring, and then it lets go, so
the blow is harder still.

237
00:17:11,305 --> 00:17:16,485
To jar down, the operator slacks off, and the stem falls
onto the tool below.

238
00:17:16,575 --> 00:17:21,500
Hundreds of blows, up and down, let a thin wire do heavy
work.

239
00:17:22,450 --> 00:17:25,315
A typical job is setting a plug.

240
00:17:25,406 --> 00:17:30,079
The plug is run in on a lock mandrel, a tool with spring-
loaded keys.

241
00:17:30,171 --> 00:17:36,254
The string is lowered past the landing nipple, then pulled
up gently, so the keys catch the groove.

242
00:17:36,345 --> 00:17:42,108
A downward jar locks them in and shears the running tool
free of the plug.

243
00:17:42,199 --> 00:17:45,192
A pull on the wire confirms it holds.

244
00:17:45,283 --> 00:17:50,793
Now the tubing below the plug is isolated, and we can work
above it.

245
00:17:50,885 --> 00:17:57,937
When the job is done, a pulling tool latches onto the
fishing neck at the top of the plug,

246
00:17:57,967 --> 00:18:00,564
and an upward jar frees the keys.

247
00:18:00,655 --> 00:18:04,008
But first, the pressure across the plug is equalised.

248
00:18:04,099 --> 00:18:08,389
Otherwise, as it lets go, it can be blown up the hole.

249
00:18:09,350 --> 00:18:12,160
A subtler job: changing a gas-lift valve.

250
00:18:12,251 --> 00:18:19,887
The valve sits in a pocket beside the main bore, so a tool
run straight down simply passes it by.

251
00:18:19,978 --> 00:18:22,338
The answer is the kick-over tool.

252
00:18:22,429 --> 00:18:28,442
It is run in with its arm folded, past the mandrel, and
then pulled slowly back up.

253
00:18:28,533 --> 00:18:33,237
A spring-loaded key catches a helical sleeve at the top of
the mandrel,

254
00:18:33,267 --> 00:18:39,469
and the helix turns the tool until the key drops into a
slot, with the arm facing the pocket.

255
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:44,186
At the top of the slot the operator sees the tension jump.

256
00:18:44,277 --> 00:18:49,314
A little more pull, and the arm kicks over and locks,
directly above the pocket.

257
00:18:49,405 --> 00:18:50,659
Now slack off.

258
00:18:50,750 --> 00:18:56,253
The pulling tool on the arm slides down into the pocket
and over the valve's latch.

259
00:18:56,344 --> 00:18:58,417
Jar down to latch on.

260
00:18:58,508 --> 00:19:05,283
Then jar up: a pin shears, the latch lets go, and the
valve comes out with the tool.

261
00:19:05,374 --> 00:19:08,950
Pulled back through the sleeve, the arm folds flat again.

262
00:19:09,041 --> 00:19:13,788
Setting a new valve is the same dance with a running tool:
kick over,

263
00:19:13,818 --> 00:19:19,773
lower the valve into the pocket, jar down to lock its
latch, and jar up to shear free.

264
00:19:19,864 --> 00:19:24,553
Pulling and setting are separate runs, so every valve
changed costs two trips.

265
00:19:25,517 --> 00:19:28,036
The same mechanics do many other jobs.

266
00:19:28,127 --> 00:19:31,466
A shifting tool opens or closes a sliding sleeve.

267
00:19:31,556 --> 00:19:34,123
A bailer scoops up sand and debris.

268
00:19:34,213 --> 00:19:40,983
A gauge cutter proves the tubing is open, and a tag run
measures how deep the well is.

269
00:19:41,073 --> 00:19:46,121
A memory gauge hangs in the well for days, recording, to
be fetched later.

270
00:19:46,211 --> 00:19:47,877
But slickline has limits.

271
00:19:47,967 --> 00:19:52,562
It cannot push, so the tool must fall under its own
weight.

272
00:19:52,653 --> 00:19:53,930
It cannot pump.

273
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:57,215
And as the well leans over, gravity runs out.

274
00:19:57,305 --> 00:20:02,641
Along the hole, the pull of the weight shrinks with the
cosine of the angle,

275
00:20:02,671 --> 00:20:05,142
while friction grows with the sine.

276
00:20:05,232 --> 00:20:11,146
With a friction coefficient of 0.3, a tool stops sliding
at about seventy-three degrees,

277
00:20:11,176 --> 00:20:15,162
and in practice the wire's own drag brings the limit
lower.

278
00:20:16,117 --> 00:20:18,740
Electric line adds power and a voice.

279
00:20:18,830 --> 00:20:23,376
Electricity goes down the conductor and measurements come
back in real time,

280
00:20:23,406 --> 00:20:26,055
so the operator watches the log being drawn.

281
00:20:26,145 --> 00:20:30,786
To know exactly where the tool is, it carries a casing
collar locator,

282
00:20:30,816 --> 00:20:37,423
which senses each joint in the casing as it passes, and
the log is tied to the known collar depths.

283
00:20:37,513 --> 00:20:40,449
The same line can fire a perforating gun.

284
00:20:40,539 --> 00:20:44,260
Each shaped charge is a metal cone packed with explosive.

285
00:20:44,350 --> 00:20:50,404
When it detonates, the cone collapses into a jet of metal
moving at several kilometres per second,

286
00:20:50,434 --> 00:20:55,091
which punches through casing, cement and a few tens of
centimetres of rock.

287
00:20:55,182 --> 00:20:59,865
Fired from the surface, at the depth the collar log gave
us.

288
00:21:00,817 --> 00:21:07,173
E-line also sets bridge plugs and packers with a setting
tool driven by a slow-burning powder charge,

289
00:21:07,203 --> 00:21:13,653
and cuts pipe with chemical or explosive cutters, work we
will need again at the end of a well's life.

290
00:21:13,745 --> 00:21:19,628
And when the well is so deviated that tools no longer
fall, a downhole tractor can be added:

291
00:21:19,658 --> 00:21:25,443
wheels or tracks that grip the wall and drive the string
along, powered through the cable.

292
00:21:25,535 --> 00:21:32,002
Beyond the reach of the tractor, in long horizontal wells,
the answer is no longer a wire at all.

293
00:21:32,093 --> 00:21:34,427
It is a pipe that can be pushed.

294
00:21:35,383 --> 00:21:42,234
So wireline is quick, light and cheap, and it is superb
for mechanical work and measurement in a live well.

295
00:21:42,326 --> 00:21:47,185
It is limited by its strength, a few tonnes at the very
most.

296
00:21:47,277 --> 00:21:50,303
It is limited by gravity in deviated wells.

297
00:21:50,395 --> 00:21:52,827
And it can neither push nor pump.

298
00:21:52,919 --> 00:21:57,525
When a job needs any of those, we reach for the next
method.

299
00:22:01,283 --> 00:22:05,091
Coiled tubing is a continuous steel pipe, with no joints,

300
00:22:05,121 --> 00:22:09,162
from one to three and a half inches across, wound on a
reel.

301
00:22:09,252 --> 00:22:14,746
One reel can hold from about one and a half to over seven
kilometres.

302
00:22:14,836 --> 00:22:20,472
It is made from flat steel strip, rolled into a tube and
welded along the seam,

303
00:22:20,502 --> 00:22:26,025
and the strips are welded end to end at a slant, so the
length is continuous.

304
00:22:26,115 --> 00:22:31,845
The steel is strong, with a yield strength of eighty
thousand pounds per square inch or more,

305
00:22:31,875 --> 00:22:34,662
yet it bends round the reel without breaking.

306
00:22:34,752 --> 00:22:38,363
And because it is a pipe, we can pump through it.

307
00:22:38,453 --> 00:22:40,069
A wireline can carry.

308
00:22:40,159 --> 00:22:43,300
A coiled tube can carry, push, and pump.

309
00:22:44,250 --> 00:22:46,793
Here is a coiled tubing spread.

310
00:22:46,892 --> 00:22:49,113
The reel stands on the deck.

311
00:22:49,213 --> 00:22:53,322
From the reel the tube rises over the gooseneck, a curved
guide,

312
00:22:53,352 --> 00:22:59,366
and straight down into the injector head, which hangs from
a crane or a frame above the tree.

313
00:22:59,465 --> 00:23:03,736
Under the injector comes the stripper, which seals around
the moving pipe,

314
00:23:03,766 --> 00:23:07,892
then a stack of blowout preventers, then the lubricator,
then the tree.

315
00:23:07,991 --> 00:23:12,938
The operator sits in a control cabin, watching pressure,
depth, weight and pump rate.

316
00:23:13,037 --> 00:23:18,886
A pump connects to the centre of the reel, and the fluid
goes out through the tube.

317
00:23:19,883 --> 00:23:22,108
The injector head is the engine.

318
00:23:22,199 --> 00:23:28,915
Two endless chains run on opposite sides of the tube, each
carrying gripper blocks shaped to its diameter.

319
00:23:29,006 --> 00:23:32,514
Hydraulic cylinders squeeze the chains together onto the
pipe.

320
00:23:32,605 --> 00:23:37,297
Turn the chains one way and the pipe is driven into the
well.

321
00:23:37,388 --> 00:23:40,351
Turn them the other way and it comes out.

322
00:23:40,442 --> 00:23:44,472
The injector can hold the pipe still against the well
pressure,

323
00:23:44,502 --> 00:23:48,239
or pull the whole weight of a long string, tens of tonnes.

324
00:23:48,330 --> 00:23:50,226
Everything depends on the grip.

325
00:23:51,183 --> 00:23:57,574
Below the injector, the stripper is a set of rubber
elements squeezed around the tube by hydraulic pressure.

326
00:23:57,743 --> 00:24:03,477
It is the dynamic seal, the same job as the stuffing box,
and like it, it wears.

327
00:24:03,645 --> 00:24:09,310
Below the stripper comes the blowout preventer, usually
with four sets of rams in a stack.

328
00:24:09,478 --> 00:24:15,101
From the top: blind rams, which seal an empty hole; shear
rams, which cut the tube;

329
00:24:15,131 --> 00:24:20,579
slip rams, which grip it so it cannot fall; and pipe rams,
which seal around it.

330
00:24:20,747 --> 00:24:24,833
In an emergency they close in sequence: pipe rams to seal,

331
00:24:24,863 --> 00:24:28,554
slips to hold, shear to cut, blind to seal the bore.

332
00:24:29,817 --> 00:24:33,937
At the end of the tube hangs the bottom hole assembly.

333
00:24:34,027 --> 00:24:37,574
First a connector, which fixes it to the tube.

334
00:24:37,665 --> 00:24:41,981
Then check valves, two flaps that only open for flow going
down,

335
00:24:42,011 --> 00:24:45,881
so that well fluid cannot come up the inside of the tube.

336
00:24:45,972 --> 00:24:50,368
Then a disconnect, which can release everything below if
it gets stuck:

337
00:24:50,398 --> 00:24:53,826
drop a ball, pump it down, and pressure parts the tool.

338
00:24:53,917 --> 00:24:58,106
Then the working tool itself: a jetting nozzle, a motor
and mill,

339
00:24:58,136 --> 00:25:01,771
an inflatable packer, a perforating gun, a logging tool.

340
00:25:01,862 --> 00:25:04,330
Same tube, different tool on the end.

341
00:25:05,283 --> 00:25:06,550
Now the physics.

342
00:25:06,642 --> 00:25:09,375
With the well open at two hundred bar,

343
00:25:09,405 --> 00:25:14,494
the pressure pushes the tube out with that force of about
four tonnes.

344
00:25:14,586 --> 00:25:21,502
At first, the tube in the hole weighs almost nothing, so
the injector has to push it in.

345
00:25:21,595 --> 00:25:25,471
As more goes in, its weight, reduced by buoyancy, grows.

346
00:25:25,564 --> 00:25:30,152
About a kilometre of two-inch tube is enough to balance
the push.

347
00:25:30,245 --> 00:25:35,996
Beyond that, the tube is heavy, and the injector holds it
back instead of pushing.

348
00:25:36,089 --> 00:25:41,285
That crossover is called the balance point, and the whole
operation is planned around it.

349
00:25:42,250 --> 00:25:45,753
Coiled tubing has an unusual enemy: it gets tired.

350
00:25:45,845 --> 00:25:52,015
Every time it comes off the reel it is bent over the
gooseneck, then straightened into the injector.

351
00:25:52,107 --> 00:25:57,457
Going in and coming out, that is at least six bends and
straightenings every trip.

352
00:25:57,549 --> 00:26:00,539
Each bend takes the steel past its yield point,

353
00:26:00,569 --> 00:26:04,553
a couple of percent of strain, so the steel flows
plastically.

354
00:26:04,645 --> 00:26:11,614
A paperclip bent back and forth breaks in a few cycles;
steel tubing lasts far longer, but not for ever.

355
00:26:11,706 --> 00:26:13,701
Internal pressure makes it worse.

356
00:26:13,793 --> 00:26:19,763
So every string has a fatigue life, tracked by software
that logs each cycle and each pressure,

357
00:26:19,793 --> 00:26:25,224
and the string is retired well before it is used up,
commonly at about eighty percent.

358
00:26:26,183 --> 00:26:28,131
The other limit is reach.

359
00:26:28,222 --> 00:26:33,844
In a horizontal well, you push the tube along the low side
of the hole.

360
00:26:33,936 --> 00:26:39,694
Friction builds along its length, and the tube feels a
compressive load, like pushing a rope.

361
00:26:39,786 --> 00:26:43,787
At a certain load the tube buckles, first into a gentle
wave,

362
00:26:43,817 --> 00:26:47,319
then into a helix that presses hard against the wall.

363
00:26:47,411 --> 00:26:53,160
The friction then climbs steeply, and however hard the
injector pushes, no more tube goes in.

364
00:26:53,252 --> 00:26:54,541
That is lock-up.

365
00:26:54,633 --> 00:26:59,019
How far you get depends strongly on friction: in our
example,

366
00:26:59,049 --> 00:27:06,433
a friction coefficient of 0.3 gives roughly fourteen
hundred metres, and 0.1 gives over four thousand.

367
00:27:06,524 --> 00:27:12,954
So engineers add lubricants, run a larger and stiffer
tube, or add a tractor or a vibrating tool.

368
00:27:13,917 --> 00:27:17,327
Because the tube is hollow, coiled tubing can circulate.

369
00:27:17,418 --> 00:27:22,436
To clean out sand, we run in with a jetting nozzle, pump
fluid down the tube,

370
00:27:22,466 --> 00:27:27,711
and the fluid returns up the annulus between tube and
tubing, carrying the sand.

371
00:27:27,802 --> 00:27:31,404
What matters is the speed of that upward flow.

372
00:27:31,495 --> 00:27:34,515
Too slow, and the sand falls out again.

373
00:27:34,605 --> 00:27:37,782
Fast enough, and it travels to the surface.

374
00:27:37,873 --> 00:27:44,213
If the reservoir is too weak to lift the fluid, we add
nitrogen to make a foam.

375
00:27:44,304 --> 00:27:48,192
And friction in a long, narrow tube limits the pump rate,

376
00:27:48,222 --> 00:27:52,896
so the size of the tube is always a trade between strength
and flow.

377
00:27:53,850 --> 00:27:55,112
Three more jobs.

378
00:27:55,203 --> 00:28:00,639
Nitrogen lift: nitrogen pumped down the tube displaces the
heavy liquid in the well.

379
00:28:00,730 --> 00:28:04,689
The column lightens, and the reservoir can start to flow
again.

380
00:28:04,781 --> 00:28:07,967
Acid placement: the nozzle sits at the right depth,

381
00:28:07,997 --> 00:28:14,240
pumping acid across the perforations while the tube is
moved up and down, so every part is treated.

382
00:28:14,331 --> 00:28:15,705
And scale removal.

383
00:28:15,796 --> 00:28:21,810
A jet of water or acid washes soft scale off the wall;
hard scale needs a mill.

384
00:28:21,902 --> 00:28:29,391
A mill is turned by a downhole motor, driven by the fluid
we pump, so the tube itself never has to rotate.

385
00:28:30,350 --> 00:28:33,892
Coiled tubing also sets plugs and packers, perforates,

386
00:28:33,922 --> 00:28:38,486
and pushes a logging tool into a horizontal well where
gravity fails.

387
00:28:38,578 --> 00:28:40,515
It places cement, and fishes.

388
00:28:40,606 --> 00:28:44,768
Hang a string inside the tubing and it becomes a velocity
string.

389
00:28:44,859 --> 00:28:48,313
Add a downhole motor and a bit and it will even drill:

390
00:28:48,343 --> 00:28:54,795
coiled tubing drilling cuts a side-track out of an
existing well, through its tubing, without a rig.

391
00:28:58,550 --> 00:29:04,449
Between coiled tubing and a rig sits the snubbing unit,
also called a hydraulic workover unit.

392
00:29:04,703 --> 00:29:07,977
It runs ordinary jointed pipe into a live well.

393
00:29:08,231 --> 00:29:12,125
Because the pipe is stiff and strong, it can be rotated,

394
00:29:12,155 --> 00:29:15,939
and it can carry far heavier loads than a coiled tube.

395
00:29:16,193 --> 00:29:21,546
The unit uses two sets of slips, one travelling on
hydraulic jacks and one fixed.

396
00:29:21,799 --> 00:29:25,841
While one grips, the other releases, the jacks stroke,

397
00:29:25,871 --> 00:29:30,094
and the pipe is pushed or pulled a few metres at a time.

398
00:29:30,347 --> 00:29:34,847
As with coiled tubing, it starts pipe-light, pushed in
against the pressure,

399
00:29:34,877 --> 00:29:37,469
and becomes pipe-heavy at the balance point,

400
00:29:37,499 --> 00:29:43,280
where the crew switches from the slips that hold the pipe
down to the slips that hold its weight.

401
00:29:45,050 --> 00:29:50,174
Sometimes the quickest way into a well is not to go in at
all.

402
00:29:50,427 --> 00:29:54,332
Bullheading is pumping fluid down the tubing from the
surface,

403
00:29:54,362 --> 00:29:58,931
hard enough to push the well fluids back into the
reservoir ahead of it.

404
00:29:59,184 --> 00:30:03,135
It can kill a well, place a scale inhibitor deep in the
rock,

405
00:30:03,165 --> 00:30:06,298
or squeeze cement and chemicals into leak paths.

406
00:30:06,552 --> 00:30:10,817
Nothing enters the well but liquid, so it is cheap and
fast.

407
00:30:11,070 --> 00:30:18,348
But it cannot be placed at a chosen depth, and the
reservoir has to be able to take the fluid.

408
00:30:19,950 --> 00:30:21,572
Finally, the heavy end.

409
00:30:21,663 --> 00:30:25,518
Some problems can only be reached by pulling the
completion:

410
00:30:25,548 --> 00:30:30,405
a leaking tubing string, a failed packer, a pump to
replace, a casing leak.

411
00:30:30,495 --> 00:30:32,652
For that, the well is killed.

412
00:30:32,743 --> 00:30:38,063
Kill-weight fluid is pumped in, and the well is checked to
make sure it holds.

413
00:30:38,154 --> 00:30:39,889
Mechanical plugs are set.

414
00:30:39,980 --> 00:30:43,915
The tree comes off and a blowout preventer goes on.

415
00:30:44,005 --> 00:30:47,618
Then the tubing is unscrewed, joint by joint, and pulled.

416
00:30:47,709 --> 00:30:55,582
The repair is made, a new completion is run, the tree goes
back on, and the well is brought back in.

417
00:30:55,673 --> 00:30:57,443
It can take weeks.

418
00:30:57,534 --> 00:31:02,660
And the kill fluid, which sits against the reservoir, may
reduce the well's productivity.

419
00:31:02,751 --> 00:31:05,758
So it is done only when it must be.

420
00:31:09,517 --> 00:31:11,561
Many Norwegian wells are subsea.

421
00:31:11,853 --> 00:31:16,471
The tree sits on the seabed, here three hundred metres
down,

422
00:31:16,501 --> 00:31:21,149
and there is no deck next to it to stand a wireline unit
on.

423
00:31:21,441 --> 00:31:27,061
So intervention here means a vessel on the surface,
connected to a tree it cannot touch.

424
00:31:27,354 --> 00:31:29,426
There are two ways to do it.

425
00:31:29,719 --> 00:31:32,135
Through open water, without a riser.

426
00:31:32,427 --> 00:31:38,321
Or inside a riser, a pipe from the vessel all the way down
to the seabed.

427
00:31:40,283 --> 00:31:43,005
How we get in depends on the tree.

428
00:31:43,345 --> 00:31:46,790
A vertical tree has the tubing hanger in the wellhead,

429
00:31:46,820 --> 00:31:51,002
below the tree, and a straight bore up through the master
valves.

430
00:31:51,342 --> 00:31:52,986
Tools go straight down.

431
00:31:53,326 --> 00:31:56,165
A horizontal tree turns that inside out.

432
00:31:56,505 --> 00:32:01,718
The tubing hanger sits inside the tree, and the oil leaves
through a side outlet,

433
00:32:01,748 --> 00:32:04,307
so the vertical bore is closed by plugs,

434
00:32:04,337 --> 00:32:09,386
which must be pulled through the equipment on top before
any tool can go down.

435
00:32:09,726 --> 00:32:13,725
A vertical tree can be lifted off without disturbing the
completion.

436
00:32:14,065 --> 00:32:17,929
A horizontal tree cannot be removed without pulling the
tubing.

437
00:32:21,183 --> 00:32:24,552
Riserless light well intervention is the cheapest way.

438
00:32:24,892 --> 00:32:28,259
A vessel with a hole through its hull, a moonpool,

439
00:32:28,289 --> 00:32:32,404
lowers a well control package onto the tree in a single
lift,

440
00:32:32,434 --> 00:32:36,103
then a lubricator with a pressure control head on top.

441
00:32:36,443 --> 00:32:40,356
The line runs through the open sea down to the stack.

442
00:32:40,696 --> 00:32:45,340
Valves in the package can close on the line, grease keeps
the seal tight,

443
00:32:45,370 --> 00:32:48,252
and glycol is injected to keep hydrates away.

444
00:32:48,592 --> 00:32:54,544
Heave compensation keeps the whole thing steady while the
vessel moves, and an ROV watches.

445
00:32:54,884 --> 00:33:00,084
The tools go down on slickline or electric line, exactly
as we saw earlier.

446
00:33:02,450 --> 00:33:07,755
When a job needs coiled tubing, or circulation, or larger
tools, we need a riser.

447
00:33:08,095 --> 00:33:12,101
A riser is a pressure-tight pipe from the vessel to the
tree,

448
00:33:12,131 --> 00:33:18,879
so the equipment on deck can be much like it would be on a
platform: a lubricator, an injector, pumps.

449
00:33:19,219 --> 00:33:24,376
At the base of the riser sits a safety package that can
close on the tool,

450
00:33:24,406 --> 00:33:28,682
shear it, and release the riser if the vessel loses
position.

451
00:33:29,022 --> 00:33:34,885
This is riser-based light well intervention, and the
vessel is a larger, more capable one.

452
00:33:38,850 --> 00:33:42,091
And at the top of the ladder is the drilling rig,

453
00:33:42,121 --> 00:33:47,596
with its large blowout preventer and marine riser, which
can do the heaviest work.

454
00:33:47,936 --> 00:33:53,124
On the Norwegian shelf, light well intervention vessels
have worked for about twenty years,

455
00:33:53,154 --> 00:33:56,996
and dedicated vessels are now contracted for most days of
the year.

456
00:33:57,336 --> 00:34:03,770
For the jobs that need a wire or a coiled tube, they are
far cheaper than a rig.

457
00:34:04,110 --> 00:34:06,424
That is the reason they exist.

458
00:34:12,417 --> 00:34:14,124
So how do we choose?

459
00:34:14,425 --> 00:34:16,466
Ask what the job needs.

460
00:34:16,557 --> 00:34:18,823
Does it need to carry and hammer?

461
00:34:19,124 --> 00:34:20,475
Slickline will do.

462
00:34:20,566 --> 00:34:23,332
Real-time data, power, or perforating?

463
00:34:23,633 --> 00:34:24,896
Electric line.

464
00:34:24,987 --> 00:34:27,528
Pumping, pushing, circulating, milling?

465
00:34:27,829 --> 00:34:28,971
Coiled tubing.

466
00:34:29,062 --> 00:34:32,039
Rotation and heavy loads in a live well?

467
00:34:32,340 --> 00:34:33,230
Snubbing.

468
00:34:33,320 --> 00:34:36,569
Or does the tubing itself have to come out?

469
00:34:36,870 --> 00:34:38,642
Then it is a rig.

470
00:34:38,732 --> 00:34:42,807
And at every step, take the lightest method that can do
the job,

471
00:34:42,837 --> 00:34:47,456
because the lighter methods are cheaper, faster, and keep
the well live.

472
00:34:48,417 --> 00:34:49,580
Three cases.

473
00:34:49,866 --> 00:34:54,566
First, a gas-lift valve has failed in a pocket at two
thousand metres.

474
00:34:54,852 --> 00:35:00,862
Wireline and a kick-over tool: one trip to pull it, one to
set the new one.

475
00:35:01,148 --> 00:35:04,237
Production is back within a day or two.

476
00:35:04,522 --> 00:35:11,034
Second, scale has narrowed the tubing and clogged the
perforations in a well leaning at fifty-five degrees.

477
00:35:11,320 --> 00:35:15,980
A wire can carry a gauge to see it, but it cannot clean
it.

478
00:35:16,265 --> 00:35:19,770
Coiled tubing, with a jetting nozzle or a mill, can.

479
00:35:20,056 --> 00:35:23,632
Third, the tubing has a leak at eighteen hundred metres.

480
00:35:23,918 --> 00:35:28,885
A patch set on electric line, or on coiled tubing, may
cover the hole.

481
00:35:29,171 --> 00:35:33,706
If it cannot, the tubing has to come out, and that is a
rig.

482
00:35:36,417 --> 00:35:38,015
The edges are moving.

483
00:35:38,122 --> 00:35:41,599
Tractors push wireline tools into horizontal wells.

484
00:35:41,706 --> 00:35:47,586
Coiled tubing carries fibre-optic cable or electric
conductors, giving it real-time data.

485
00:35:47,692 --> 00:35:54,055
Vessels do work that once needed rigs, and operators are
plugging subsea wells with vessels where they can.

486
00:35:54,161 --> 00:35:58,938
But the principle is the same as it ever was: a pressure-
tight airlock,

487
00:35:58,968 --> 00:36:03,166
a way to carry and place tools, and two barriers at all
times.

488
00:36:04,183 --> 00:36:05,818
Why do we intervene?

489
00:36:06,151 --> 00:36:11,531
To see, to secure, to clear, to stimulate, to lift, to
steer, and to finish.

490
00:36:11,654 --> 00:36:12,393
How?

491
00:36:12,727 --> 00:36:18,238
With a wire that can carry and hammer, a tube that can
also pump and push,

492
00:36:18,268 --> 00:36:23,359
a pipe that can rotate, or a rig that can pull the whole
well apart.

493
00:36:23,482 --> 00:36:28,749
A producing well is only as healthy as the last time
someone looked inside it.

494
00:36:29,873 --> 00:36:31,467
That is well intervention.

